From a83d04f22e9b385dcf4c4d15872fa2f259f3d2d6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Campos Ramos <Capitain_Jack@yahoo.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 19:02:09 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] Show a side-by-side pair to the two eyes with a "Stereo 3D"
 canvas

A document that is a side-by-side pair (at least twice as wide as high,
or marked "stereo", "3D" or "side by side" in its title, subject,
keywords or description, "right eye first" for the other eye order) can
be shown as one eye's picture: View > Stereo 3D paints the left half in
the left eye's buffer and the right half in the right eye's buffer, at
the same place, while the checkers, borders, decorations and the brush
outline are drawn once and are the same in both eyes. View > Paint on
the Right View chooses the half the strokes land on (the left one by
default), the pointer maps to the half that is shown.

Qt takes the left and right buffers when the window is created, so the
setting ("stereo3DCanvas" in kritadisplayrc) is read at start-up: it asks
for a stereo surface, keeps the canvas on Qt's xcb platform (Qt's Wayland
platform has no stereo buffers) and a message says when the change needs
a restart. A surface without stereo buffers shows the half that is
painted on. Off by default; with it off nothing is drawn differently.

The converter shows one half of the image (setStereoView) and, for the
frame of the other eye, maps the other half to the same place
(beginStereoEyeView). The menu file version goes to 545 for the two new
actions.

Assisted-by: Claude Sonnet via Claude Code
